# xv6-AdaptiveSched — Adaptive Time-Slicing & Process Statistics for xv6

A modified [xv6](https://github.com/mit-pdos/xv6-public) (MIT's teaching UNIX kernel) that replaces the stock fixed-quantum Round Robin scheduler with **per-process, adaptive time-slicing** and adds a **kernel-level process statistics & fairness monitor**. Built as a CSE323 (Operating Systems) project.

> Suggested repo name: **`xv6-adaptive-scheduler`** (clearer than `xv6_cse323` if you want the name to describe what the project actually does — kept the course code as a subtitle below.)
> Course: CSE323 · Base: xv6-public (MIT)

---

## 🎯 Why

Stock xv6 schedules every process with the exact same, fixed one-tick-per-turn Round Robin quantum. It can't tell a CPU-bound process from an I/O-bound one, has no historical runtime data, and gives you no way to see *why* the scheduler behaves the way it does. This project turns xv6 into a small, **observable and tunable** scheduler you can actually experiment with:

- **Feature 1 — Per-Process Time-Slice Customization**: processes get a workload-aware default quantum (10 ticks normal / 5 ticks for detected CPU-bound processes) and can be re-tuned live at runtime.
- **Feature 2 — Process Statistics & Fairness Monitor**: exposes CPU ticks, sleep ticks, runnable-queue ticks, context switches, and preemptions per process, plus derived fairness metrics (CPU-share, wait-ratio, preempt-ratio).

---

## ✨ Features

### 1. Per-Process Time-Slice Customization
- Every process starts with a **default 10-tick quantum**; processes the scheduler detects as CPU-intensive automatically drop to a **5-tick quantum**, reducing their dominance of the CPU without starving them.
- Time slices can be **changed live** for any PID via a new syscall + user command.
- A new `ps`-style command lists every active PID with its current timeslice.

### 2. Advanced Process Statistics & Fairness Monitor
- Tracks five raw runtime metrics per process directly in the kernel:
  - CPU execution ticks
  - Sleep ticks (I/O wait)
  - Runnable-queue (ready but waiting) ticks
  - Context switches
  - Preemptions
- Derives three fairness metrics from the raw stats:
  - **CPU-share** — % of total tracked time spent running
  - **Wait-ratio** — runnable ticks relative to CPU ticks
  - **Preempt-ratio** — preemptions relative to total context switches

---

## 🧩 New System Calls

| Syscall | Number | Purpose | Exposed via |
|---|---|---|---|
| `setquantum_pid(pid, quantum)` | 23 | Set a custom timeslice (1–100 ticks) for a given PID | `sq` |
| `gettimeslice(pid)` | 24 | Read the current timeslice for a given PID | `ps` |
| `getpstats(pid, &pstats)` | 25 | Read the 5 raw runtime metrics for a given PID into a `struct pstats` | `pstats` |

## 🧪 New User Programs

| Program | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `sq.c` | `sq <pid> <quantum>` — sets PID's timeslice at runtime (validates 1–100 range) |
| `ps.c` | Lists all active PIDs with their current timeslice |
| `pstats.c` | `pstats <pid>` — prints raw stats + computed CPU-share / wait-ratio / preempt-ratio |
| `cpuhog.c` | Infinite busy loop used to simulate a CPU-bound process for testing |

---

## ⚙️ Kernel Changes

| File | Change |
|---|---|
| `proc.h` | Added `timeslice`, `ticks_used`, `io_wait_time` to `struct proc`; added new `struct pstats` (cpu_ticks, sleep_ticks, runnable_ticks, ctx_switches, preemptions), embedded in `struct proc` |
| `proc.c` | `allocproc()` initializes `timeslice=10`, `ticks_used=0`, `io_wait_time=0`; same defaults applied in `fork()`. `scheduler()`/`sleep()` updated to increment context switches and sleep ticks, and to auto-shrink the timeslice for detected CPU-bound processes |
| `trap.c` | Timer-interrupt handler increments CPU ticks, tracks `ticks_used` against the process's quantum, force-`yield()`s when the quantum is exhausted, and increments the preemption counter |
| `sysproc.c` | Kernel-side syscall handlers (`sys_setquantum_pid`, `sys_gettimeslice`, `sys_getpstats`) bridging user calls to the proc-table fields |
| `syscall.c` / `syscall.h` | New syscall numbers 23–25 registered and wired into the syscall table |
| `user.h` / `usys.S` | User-space prototypes and syscall stubs for the three new calls |
| `Makefile` | New user programs (`sq`, `ps`, `pstats`, `cpuhog`) added to the build |

---

## 📊 Evaluation Summary

Testing used `ps` (baseline timeslices), `cpuhog` (to simulate CPU-bound load), `sq` (runtime re-tuning), and `pstats` (fairness metrics), sampled before/after running CPU-intensive processes.

**Timeslice assignment**

| Process type | PID(s) | Default timeslice | Behavior |
|---|---|---|---|
| Normal | 1, 2 | 10 ticks | Responsive; adjustable via `sq` |
| CPU-bound (`cpuhog`) | 6, 7 | 5 ticks | Automatically shortened |

**Fairness metrics for a sustained CPU-bound process (PID 6)**

| Metric | Value | Interpretation |
|---|---|---|
| CPU-share | 82% | Process dominates CPU time |
| Wait-ratio | 20% | Minimal ready-queue waiting |
| Preempt-ratio | 98% | Scheduler is actively taking the CPU back |

**vs. stock xv6**

| Indicator | Default xv6 | This implementation | Change |
|---|---|---|---|
| Max wait time | >200 ticks | 45 ticks | −77.5% |
| Context-switch overhead | ~12% of CPU | ~8% of CPU | −33% |
| Interactive responsiveness | Delayed | Prompt | ✅ |
| CPU-bound process's CPU share | ~95% | 82% | Fairer sharing with other processes |

---

## 🚀 Building & Running

Standard xv6-public build/run flow (QEMU):

```bash
make qemu        # build kernel + fs image and boot in QEMU
# or
make qemu-nox    # boot without the QEMU GUI (console only)
```

Once booted, try the new commands from the xv6 shell:

```sh
ps                  # list PIDs with current timeslices
cpuhog &             # start a CPU-bound background process, note its PID
ps                   # confirm it dropped to a 5-tick timeslice
sq 6 30              # manually set PID 6's timeslice to 30 ticks
pstats 6              # view raw stats + fairness metrics for PID 6
```

Requires the usual xv6 toolchain (`qemu`, an `i386`/`x86`-capable cross-compiler or GCC with `-m32` support, `make`). See the original xv6 `README` in this repo for full toolchain setup instructions.

---

## ⚠️ Known Limitations / Future Work

- **No timeslice inheritance** — child processes currently start at the default quantum rather than inheriting an adjusted quantum from their parent.
- **No priority system** — scheduling is fairness/quantum-based only; there's no priority tier layered on top yet.
- Planned: hierarchical timeslice inheritance (children inherit a parent's adjusted quantum) and priority-aware scheduling on top of the current adaptive-quantum mechanism, to get closer to real-world OS scheduler behavior.

